  "status": "Dismissed",
  "actual_commencement_date": null,
  "decision_conditions": [
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposed development represents an isolated, out of character addition to Blackberry Close and one which would not constitute a form of character-led intensification. This is contrary to the strategic objective of Policy 8 of the adopted Waltham Forest Local Plan LP1 (2024), which seeks to ensure that the character of residential areas is reinforced. The principle of development is therefore unacceptable.&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;The submitted documents fail to fully address the effects on two mature trees directly adjacent to the application site. Consequently the true impact of the development in terms of biodiversity and trees is undetermined. This may lead to a loss of mature trees to implement the development, which provide important visual amenity to nearby residential properties as well as a contribution towards local habitat. This is harmful to both biodiversity and visual amenity and contrary to the aims and objectives of Policies 79 and 80 of the adopted Waltham Forest Local Plan LP1 (2024).&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;The application comprises of inaccurate plans and elevations, which are incorrectly scaled. As such it is not possible to fully assess the proposal in terms of whether it meets residential space standards. Consequently it is undetermined whether the proposal provides adequate living conditions for future occupiers, which is to the detriment of continued enjoyment of the dwelling. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policy 56 of the adopted Waltham Forest Local Plan LP1 (2024).&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;In the absence of a legal agreement, the proposal fails to provide the necessary financial contributions to secure mitigation of the development against the Epping Forest Special Area of Conservation. It therefore fails to comply with Policy 81 of the adopted Waltham Forest Local Plan LP1 (2024) and the Supplementary Planning Document - Planning Obligations (2017).&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;Additionally in the absence of a legal agreement to restrict occupiers in obtaining on-street parking permits, the proposal constitutes unsustainable development in that it does not promote sustainable transport. Furthermore given the uncontrolled nature of the unadopted highway on which it would be located, this presents opportunity to contribute further towards unsafe access for pedestrian movements. Therefore this conflicts with the objectives of Policies 61 and 66 of the adopted Waltham Forest Local Plan LP1 (2024), which seek to improve the pedestrian environment and secure car-free development.&lt;/p&gt;"
  ],
